2. Typed observation algebra

2.1 Base sets and measurement states

Let \mathcal{S} be botanical taxa, \mathcal{G} geographic units, \mathcal{Z} agroecological zones, \mathcal{K} cultivars, \mathcal{E} maturity stages, \mathcal{D} storage conditions, \mathcal{N} nutrient identifiers (INFOODS tagnames), \mathcal{U} UCUM unit codes, \mathcal{M} analytical methods, \Pi=\{\text{field},\text{GH-cold},\text{GH-heated},\text{hydroponic},\text{imported}\} production systems, and \mathcal{T} a discrete time domain.

A measurement is not a number with an optional absence flag. It is a value of a sum type that makes the measurement state explicit and non-collapsible:

\mathsf{M} \;=\; \mathsf{Obs}(v,u,\sigma,d) \;\mid\; \mathsf{Cen}(l,h,\tau,L) \;\mid\; \mathsf{Est}(v,u,\mu,\sigma) \;\mid\; \mathsf{Miss}(r) \;\mid\; \mathsf{NA}(j),

where v\in\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}, u\in\mathcal{U}, \sigma is a standard uncertainty component, d detection metadata; (l,h) are interval bounds with censoring type \tau\in\{\text{left},\text{right},\text{interval}\} and detection limit L; \mu is a model identifier; and

r\in\mathcal{R}=\{\mathrm{NR},\mathrm{NM},\mathrm{NU},\mathrm{WH},\mathrm{SU}\}

denotes not reported, not measured, not available, withheld, and source unavailable. The distinction between \mathrm{NR} and \mathrm{NM} is retained because it bears on whether the information could in principle be recovered.

The justification j of \mathsf{NA}(j) must be defined rather than left implicit. It distinguishes not applicable because the analyte cannot exist in the food from not applicable for other reasons. This is established practice in food composition: FAO/INFOODS uses flag M for “missing because the value cannot exist” and flag O for other missing reasons, and explicitly warns that a value that cannot exist must not be replaced by zero. The framework therefore sets

j\in\{\text{cannot-exist},\text{not-compiled},\text{suppressed}\}.

An observation is a tuple

o=\big(s,g,z,\tau_h,\tau_m,\pi,\kappa,\varepsilon,\delta,\nu,m,\mathsf{M},q_{\mathrm{obs}},P\big),

with \tau_h,\tau_m\in\mathcal{T}\cup\{\bot\}, q_{\mathrm{obs}} an observation-quality vector (§6.2), and P a provenance graph reference (§5.1).

2.2 Typed partial arithmetic and the closure property

Let \boxplus\in\{+,-,\times,\div\} be a binary operation on compatible dimensions. The earlier formulation claimed a universal “no silent zero” property, that there is no pair (x,y) with x\notin\{\mathsf{Obs}(0)\} such that x\boxplus y=0. That universal claim is false: \mathsf{Obs}(5)\times\mathsf{Obs}(0)=\mathsf{Obs}(0), and \mathsf{Obs}(3)-\mathsf{Obs}(3)=\mathsf{Obs}(0). The defensible and useful property is a closure property: no operation synthesizes a numeric constructor from a non-numeric one.

Proposition 2.1 (Closure). For \boxplus\in\{+,\times\} on non-negative values:

1. If x+y=\mathsf{Obs}(0), then x=y=\mathsf{Obs}(0).
2. If x\times y=\mathsf{Obs}(0), then x=\mathsf{Obs}(0) or y=\mathsf{Obs}(0).
3. For every r,j and every x, \mathsf{Miss}(r)\boxplus x=\mathsf{Miss}(r) and \mathsf{NA}(j)\boxplus x=\mathsf{NA}(j).
4. No rule maps \mathsf{Miss} or \mathsf{NA} to a numeric constructor.

Proof. By case analysis on the constructors. For (1), non-negative a+b=0 implies a=b=0. For (2), ab=0 implies a=0 or b=0. For (3), \mathsf{Miss} and \mathsf{NA} are absorbing by definition. For (4), the only rules that introduce a numeric constructor are those with \mathsf{Obs} or \mathsf{Cen} premises. \square

This replaces the earlier, weaker claim. It retains the design rule that zero means a measured zero, while admitting that algebraic operations can legitimately produce zero from non-zero operands.

2.3 Subtraction, division, and signed intervals

The declared codomain v\in\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is not closed under subtraction. Three options are available: a signed value type, an explicit rule that subtraction yielding a negative result returns \mathsf{Miss}(\mathrm{NU}) with a dimensional annotation, or a restriction of \boxplus to operations that preserve non-negativity. For the pilot, the second option is adopted:

\mathsf{Obs}(a)-\mathsf{Obs}(b)=
\begin{cases}
\mathsf{Obs}(a-b,u), & a\ge b,\\
\mathsf{Miss}(\mathrm{NU})\text{ with annotation}, & a<b.
\end{cases}

Interval subtraction must be stated separately rather than through a generic \boxplus:

\mathsf{Obs}(a)-\mathsf{Cen}(l,h)=\mathsf{Cen}(a-h,\;a-l),

with the same non-negativity guard. Division requires b>0 for \mathsf{Obs}(b); division by a \mathsf{Cen} interval containing zero is undefined and returns \mathsf{Miss}(\mathrm{NU}).

2.4 Est and NA

\mathsf{Est}(v,u,\mu,\sigma) propagates like \mathsf{Obs} for + and \times, with the model identifier carried forward and the uncertainty treated as model-tagged:

\mathsf{Est}(a)\boxplus\mathsf{Obs}(b)=\mathsf{Est}(a\boxplus b,u_{\mathrm{comb}},\mu),

and it does not absorb numeric states. It is absorbed by \mathsf{Miss} and \mathsf{NA}. \mathsf{NA}(j) is absorbing in the same way as \mathsf{Miss}(r), but its justification is preserved; when two distinct justifications meet, the result carries the union of justifications rather than a single collapsed token.

2.5 Uncertainty components

The earlier notation conflated analytical uncertainty with between-sample variability. In the Phillips retail study, for example, three sampling occasions per season yield means whose dispersion reflects between-sample and between-occasion variation, not the analytical precision of the HPLC method. The framework therefore separates

\sigma^2=\sigma_{\mathrm{anal}}^2+\sigma_{\mathrm{sample}}^2+\sigma_{\mathrm{occasion}}^2,

and requires the uncertainty vector to identify which components are reported. For a differentiable f(x,y) with independent inputs,

u_f^2\approx\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)^2u_x^2+\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\right)^2u_y^2.

For the fresh-to-dry conversion v_{\mathrm{dry}}=v_{\mathrm{fresh}}/(1-w),

u_{\mathrm{dry}}^2\approx\frac{u_v^2}{(1-w)^2}+\frac{v_{\mathrm{fresh}}^2\,u_w^2}{(1-w)^4}.

A converted value must never appear more precise than the measurements that generated it.

