3. The four seasonality domains and partial identification

3.1 Typing

The four domains are distinct mathematical objects, not four copies of one map:

\mathcal{B}(s,g,z,\pi,\kappa,y)\quad\text{(harvest window or harvest probability)},

\mathcal{M}(p,g,t,c)\quad\text{(commercial availability of product \(p\) in market \(g\) at time \(t\) through channel \(c\))},

\mathcal{N}(s,\nu,g,t,\kappa,\varepsilon,\delta,m)\quad\text{(distribution of nutrient \(\nu\) with measurement basis)},

\mathcal{E}(p,c,t,FU,B,I)\quad\text{(environmental impact for functional unit \(FU\), system boundary \(B\), impact category \(I\))}.

\mathcal{B} is a production constraint; \mathcal{M} a commercial observation; \mathcal{N} a statistical property of measurements; \mathcal{E} an impact assessment.

3.2 From non-recoverability to partial identification

The earlier Proposition 3.1 used a counterexample to conclude that no function of \mathcal{M} recovers \mathcal{B}. A counterexample establishes that the identity \mathcal{B}=\mathcal{M} fails; it does not establish non-identifiability. The correct formulation is a partial identification result.

Let W_1 and W_2 be two worlds with the same distribution of market availability M but different harvest windows B. Then B is not point-identified from M. However, under explicit assumptions one can derive bounds. Suppose produce is sold only within a maximum storage-and-transport lag S, and let t be the purchase time. Then

B\subseteq[t-S,\;t].

Under an additional assumption that no product is stored longer than S, the feasible harvest window is bounded below by t-S. The bound is testable against external harvest calendars, and it is the kind of statement a provenance-preserving system should record: not “the harvest date is t,” but “the harvest date lies in [t-S,t] under assumption A.” The same logic separates \mathcal{N} from \mathcal{M}: nutrient concentration depends on cultivar, maturity, storage, and method, none of which is a function of purchase date, but conditional bounds may still be derivable.

3.3 Separation invariant

An observation is seasonally well-formed iff the recording system stores \mathcal{B}, \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{N}, and \mathcal{E} as distinct attributes, and no inference rule populates one from another. This is enforced as a validation invariant, not a convention.

