4. Semantic identification

4.1 Three identity levels

Taxonomic identity uses WCVP accepted names with authority and version. Agricultural identity records cultivar or genotype, or \mathrm{UNKNOWN}. Food identity uses a FoodOn concept IRI. The source name is always preserved alongside the normalized identity.

4.2 Taxonomic scope and crosswalk

WCVP covers vascular plants only: flowering plants, conifers, ferns, clubmosses, and firmosses. FoodOn, by contrast, spans plants, animals, fungi, and algae and imports NCBITaxon. An explicit NCBITaxon↔WCVP crosswalk is therefore required, and fish and fungi—both strongly seasonal—fall outside the WCVP domain as written. The framework must either declare them out of scope or introduce a second taxonomic authority for those kingdoms. This is a scope limitation of the first order, not a detail.

4.3 Unit harmonization is UCUM plus measurement basis

UCUM supplies syntax and dimensional semantics. It does not encode fresh versus dry basis, edible portion, raw versus cooked state, analyte definition, chemical equivalent, or moisture. Two records with identical UCUM units may therefore be scientifically incomparable. The harmonization stage carries a measurement-basis vector

\beta=(\text{basis},\text{edible portion},\text{state},\text{analyte},\text{moisture},\text{serving basis}).

Dimensional coercion is permitted only when \dim(u)=\dim(u') and the basis vectors are equal or related by a documented, cultivar-independent transformation. The conversion factor, the basis transformation, and the propagated uncertainty are stored as transformation parameters.

Analyte identity belongs in the INFOODS tagname and should not be duplicated in \beta. For vitamin C, INFOODS VITC is the sum of L-ascorbic acid (ASCL) and L-dehydroascorbic acid (ASCDL). HPLC may measure both, whereas titration measures ASCL only. Recording nutrient: INFOODS:VITC and method: HPLC is sufficient; the analyte definition is carried by the tagname, and duplicating it in \beta creates a consistency hazard.

