5. Provenance and reconstruction

5.1 Provenance graph and immutable events

A provenance graph is a directed labeled graph P=(V,E,\lambda) aligned with PROV-O, with entities, activities, and agents, and edges labeled used, wasGeneratedBy, wasAttributedTo, wasDerivedFrom, and wasRevisionOf. Each stage emits an append-only event

e=(\mathrm{id},\mathrm{id}_{\mathrm{in}},\mathrm{id}_{\mathrm{out}},h_{\mathrm{in}},h_{\mathrm{out}},t_{\mathrm{id}},v_{\mathrm{sw}},\pi_{\mathrm{params}},\mathrm{ts},\mathrm{agent},\mathrm{status},\rho),

with content hashes h, transformation identifier t_{\mathrm{id}}, software version v_{\mathrm{sw}}, parameters \pi_{\mathrm{params}}, and reversibility status \rho. Corrections create new versions; historical records are never overwritten. This is consistent with the PROV-O versioning pattern, in which a mutable resource is connected to immutable revision entities rather than mutated in place.

5.2 Verifiable replay, not a deep induction

The earlier “provenance-complete reconstruction” theorem was framed as an induction over transformations. Given that the original payload S_o is retained, the induction adds little: one can recover o by re-parsing S_o. The substantive claim is not invertibility but verifiable replay.

Theorem 5.2 (Verifiable replay). Let S_o be an immutable source payload, E=(e_1,\dots,e_n) a finite event log, and R a deterministic replay function that applies the recorded transformations to S_o in event order. Suppose:

· (A1) S_o is retained as an immutable entity;
· (A2) every event records input and output identifiers, parameters, software version, and configuration;
· (A3) numeric representations preserve sufficient precision, or record the permitted error \epsilon;
· (A4) the chain is acyclic and resolves to S_o.

Then R(E,S_o)=T(o) and h(R(E,S_o))=h(T(o)), within \epsilon where (A3) permits tolerance.

Proof. Determinism of R and (A2) give equality of the computed output with the recorded output; (A1) and (A4) give access to the input; (A3) bounds numerical deviation. The hash equality is a testable consequence. \square

The real risk is not the induction but the parsing step: the potato error in the pilot—copying a pooled winter/spring value into two seasonal columns—entered at parsing, not at transformation. Content-addressed locators mitigate this risk.

5.3 Provenance-preserving representation

Define A(o)=(T(o),\mathrm{ID}_o,P_o,S_o). The map \mathrm{ID}\mapsto S must be well-defined, not injective. A source document contains many observations, so an identifier must include a locator—table, row, column, cell—and ideally a content hash of the located payload. Under well-definedness and locator integrity,

A(o_1)=A(o_2)\;\Rightarrow\;o_1=o_2

within the same locator domain. Non-loss attaches to the augmented archival representation, not to normalized values alone. Normalization may intentionally collapse synonymous representations into a canonical identifier; this is not information loss provided the original identifier and source payload are preserved.

5.4 Conditional confluence rather than unconditional commutation

Taxonomic normalization and unit normalization act on disjoint fields, so they commute trivially when the disjointness condition holds. The earlier proposition is therefore near-vacuous in its interesting case. The substantive question is what happens when the conditions fail—for example, when a fresh-to-dry conversion depends on cultivar-specific moisture content.

Model the transformation system as a rewrite system. A critical pair arises when two transformations apply to overlapping fields. Local confluence of all critical pairs, together with termination, gives confluence by Newman’s lemma. The practical rule is:

· If T_{\mathrm{tax}} and T_{\mathrm{unit}} act on disjoint fields and neither reads the other’s output, they commute.
· If a conversion factor depends on a field written by the other transformation, record the dependency edge and the actual order. Do not assert commutation; assert conditional confluence on the subdomain where the dependency is absent.

This is testable: enumerate critical pairs, check local confluence, and report any pair that fails. No category-theoretic functoriality claim is needed.

