6. Quality and evidence

6.1 Three assessment structures

Observation quality evaluates a single measurement. Dataset quality evaluates a collection. Proposition evidence evaluates a scientific claim. A precise analytical measurement does not constitute clinical evidence; a systematic review may rest on poorly documented individual measurements.

6.2 Observation quality vector

q_{\mathrm{obs}}=(q_{\mathrm{id}},q_{\mathrm{samp}},q_{\mathrm{method}},q_{\mathrm{prec}},q_{\mathrm{repr}},q_{\mathrm{prov}}),

covering food-identity confidence, sampling documentation, analytical method, measurement precision, representativeness, and provenance completeness. A single ordinal label may be displayed for convenience but is not the stored representation. Aggregation for display must be documented; otherwise the “calibrated summary” claim is unsupported.

6.3 Dataset quality

Representativeness, coverage, internal consistency, and compilation procedure. This assessment attaches to a released dataset version, not to individual observations.

6.4 Proposition evidence

e_{\mathrm{prop}}=(d,k,\beta,\hat{\theta},\mathrm{CI},I^2,\rho_{\mathrm{rep}},c),

covering study design, number of independent studies, risk of bias, effect estimate, confidence interval, heterogeneity, replication status, and conclusion. This attaches to a proposition identifier, not to an observation.

6.5 What is and is not claimed

The three structures are conceptually distinct and separately reported. It is not claimed that observation quality and proposition evidence are statistically independent; that would require a proof not currently available. Institutional-database membership does not by itself confer validated status; missing information is not a low-quality measurement; a single observational study does not satisfy an independence criterion; and the existence of a systematic review does not by itself establish high confidence.

