7. Inference architecture

7.1 Four-outcome classification rebuilt from equivalence bounds

Let \theta be the effect of interest, \Delta>0 the minimum scientifically relevant effect in the benefit direction, and [-\Delta,\Delta] the equivalence interval. The classification is defined by the confidence interval [\ell,u] relative to these bounds:

Classification Criterion
Supported (benefit) \ell>\Delta
Supported (harm) u<-\Delta, when harm is a relevant directional outcome
Practically equivalent [-\Delta,\Delta] contains [\ell,u] (TOST)
Unresolved none of the above

“Significant but practically trivial” is not a separate class: it is either equivalent, if the interval lies inside the margins, or unresolved, if the interval straddles \Delta. “Not supported” as a standalone category is removed because it overlaps with equivalence and with unresolved. The fourth category is not a failure of research; it is an honest statement about the available information.

7.2 Anchoring \Delta and power for TOST

Phillips et al. judged differences of at least 10% of the daily reference intake (90 mg vitamin C) as meaningful. This yields a per-serving anchor:

\Delta_{\mathrm{serving}}=0.10\times 90\ \mathrm{mg}=9\ \mathrm{mg}.

For a ratio-scale analysis, the margin is applied on the log scale. For a two-group TOST with equal group sizes n, true difference zero, variance \sigma^2, and equivalence margin \Delta, the approximate per-group size is

n\approx\frac{2\sigma^2\left(z_{1-\alpha}+z_{1-\beta/2}\right)^2}{\Delta^2}.

The earlier formula used z_{1-\beta}, which is the superiority-test power term. For TOST, both one-sided tests must be significant, and the power term is z_{1-\beta/2} in the equal-margin case; exact calculations use a noncentral t distribution. The choice of \Delta is fixed on scientific grounds before data collection, not after.

7.3 H1 model with environmental covariates and censoring-aware likelihood

The H1 statement is: season-associated differences in nutrient composition exist for specified species, regions, and production systems, conditional on measured cultivar, maturity, storage, and analytical factors. The model is

\begin{aligned}
Y_{ijklmn}={}&\beta_0+f(\text{temperature},\text{radiation},\text{degree-days})+\beta_1\kappa+\beta_2\varepsilon+\beta_3\delta+\beta_4 m+\beta_5\pi\\
&+\beta_6(\kappa\times\text{environment})+\beta_7(\pi\times\text{environment})+u_{\mathrm{site}}+u_{\mathrm{study}}+\epsilon.
\end{aligned}

Season labels are replaced by continuous environmental covariates because crude season proxies are heterogeneous across latitudes and agroecological zones. With one or two years of data, the year variance component is not estimable; year enters as a fixed effect or is dropped. The literature reports seasonal or meteorological nutrient change in the great majority of mapped studies, mostly over one to two years, which raises a publication-bias concern and further motivates modelling environmental drivers rather than labels.

Censored observations enter through a censoring-aware likelihood, not through substitution. For a left-censored observation at limit L, the likelihood contribution is

\Pr(Y\le L)=\Phi\!\left(\frac{L-\mu}{\sigma}\right),

and for an interval (l,h),

\Pr(l<Y\le h)=\Phi\!\left(\frac{h-\mu}{\sigma}\right)-\Phi\!\left(\frac{l-\mu}{\sigma}\right).

Maximum likelihood and robust regression on order statistics are the established choices; substitution of L/2 is known to be biased. The vitamin C pilot contains no censored or Est cases, so it never exercises half of the algebra. This is a limitation of the pilot, not a defect of the algebra.

7.4 H2 descoped

Seasonal intake variation is inconsistent across studies, so the exposure is ill-defined. H2 is descoped from the present work. A defensible reformulation would be a mediation chain ending in a measured biomarker—for example, seasonal availability → intake → plasma vitamin C—with an explicit identification strategy and sensitivity analysis. That is a separate study.

7.5 H3 and the trade-off outcome

For impact vector I(p,c)=(I_1,\dots,I_m),

c_s\preceq c_y\iff I_i(p,c_s)\le I_i(p,c_y)\ \forall i,
\qquad
c_s\prec c_y\iff c_s\preceq c_y\text{ and }\exists i:I_i(p,c_s)<I_i(p,c_y).

If c_s\prec c_y, seasonal sourcing dominates. If c_y\prec c_s, it is dominated. Otherwise the outcome is a trade-off, which is a substantive result and not a failed calculation.

The literature supports the trade-off outcome rather than a universal ranking. In a Morocco→France tomato comparison, the Moroccan product used 28.0 L H₂Oeq kg⁻¹ versus 7.5 L H₂Oeq kg⁻¹ for the French product, so local production performed better on water, while the energy ranking differed. In Austrian horticulture, heated greenhouses emitted roughly twice what Spanish and Italian imports did, showing that the production system can dominate transport. A Morocco→France corridor pilot with water use in I is therefore a ready-made test of the Pareto trade-off classification.

