8. The seven-stage pipeline

1. Source acquisition. Original records, source versions, URLs, access dates, licensing, immutable snapshots.
2. Source preservation. Original fields, units, taxonomic names, values, and identifiers retained without modification.
3. Semantic identification. Taxonomic identity (WCVP plus NCBITaxon crosswalk), agricultural identity, food identity (FoodOn), nutrient identity (INFOODS), measurement basis.
4. Typed unit harmonization. UCUM syntax, dimensional compatibility, basis transformation, conversion factors, uncertainty propagation.
5. Seasonality classification. \mathcal{B}, \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{N}, \mathcal{E} recorded separately, with the separation invariant enforced.
6. Quality and evidence assessment. Three structures per §6.
7. Validation and publication. Verifiable replay, confluence tests, audit report, versioned records, machine-readable export.

The store is append-only at observation and provenance level. Every stage emits an immutable event. Validation comprises exact replay of the source representation, replay of normalized values within \epsilon, dimensional safety, provenance reachability, separation-invariant compliance, and separate reporting of the three assessment structures.

The pipeline interoperates with PROV-O for provenance, UCUM for units, FoodOn for food concepts, WCVP for botanical nomenclature (with the scope caveat of §4.2), INFOODS and EuroFIR for composition standards, USDA Foundation Foods for sample-level metadata conventions, and METROFOOD-IT for a reference model of notarized agrifood data integrity. METROFOOD-IT uses selective, hash-only on-chain anchoring: only cryptographic hashes of selected assets are recorded on the ledger, while full datasets remain off-chain. The Atlas should anchor Merkle roots of record batches, not individual records, to preserve the cost and privacy properties of that architecture.

