9. Pilot specification

9.1 Source correction and attribution

The primary pilot source is Phillips et al. (2018), a primary retail-sampling study conducted in Blacksburg, Virginia, United States, not France. Fresh produce was sampled on three occasions per season from local retail outlets for one or two years, with vitamin C by HPLC, reported in mg kg⁻¹ and converted here to mg/100 g. The earlier worked record's geography: FRA is incorrect and is replaced by {country: USA, site: Blacksburg VA}.

Spinach values are 436, 298, and 180 mg kg⁻¹ for winter, spring, and summer/fall, i.e. 43.6, 29.8, and 18.0 mg/100 g. Potato is 156 mg kg⁻¹ in summer/fall versus 106 mg kg⁻¹ in winter/spring pooled; the pooled value must not be copied into two seasonal columns, because doing so invents a spring observation and violates the no-synthesis rule. The potato record is represented as a pooled winter/spring state, not as two states.

The broccoli increase of 157.46% in summer–autumn relative to winter–spring derives from Shi et al. (2024), Plants 13(10):1329, DOI 10.3390/plants13101329. Winter–spring heads had higher dry matter, so the fresh-basis headline changes under a dry-basis comparison. A different study found ascorbic acid highest in winter-grown broccoli. The effect is therefore region × cultivar × cycle, not “season” as such. The broccoli record carries basis: fresh and basis: dry as separate measurement states, with moisture documented.

A third independent source is the UC Davis commercial broccoli study: 80 samples over two years, vitamin C 57.35–131.35 mg/100 g fresh weight. This is registered with its own design, geography, cultivar information, and analytical method; it is not merged with Shi et al.

9.2 Corrected worked record

```
record_id:       a3f7-...-b1c2
taxon:           Spinacia oleracea (WCVP accepted, version pinned)
cultivar:        UNKNOWN
food:            FoodOn spinach, raw, edible portion
geography:       {country: USA, site: Blacksburg VA}
seasonality:     {harvest_date: Miss(NR), production: field,
                  sampling_season: winter, harvest_window: [t-S, t]}
nutrient:        INFOODS:VITC
value:           Obs(43.6, mg/100g, σ_sample, d)
method:          HPLC
provenance:      prov:graph/0e4d...
q_obs:           (id: moderate, samp: retail-seasonal, method: HPLC,
                  prec: between-sample, repr: single market, prov: complete)
```

The label harvest_date: Miss(NR) is correct: the study does not report harvest date, and retail sampling season is not a substitute. The proposition supported by this record is a sampling-season association in this market, not H1 as stated. H1 requires observed harvest time, which the pilot does not provide. The pilot validates the pipeline and exercises the separation invariant; it does not test H1.

