B.2 Proof of Proposition 2.1 (closure), exhaustive

Proposition (Closure). For \boxplus\in\{+,\times\} on non-negative values:

1. If x+y=\mathsf{Obs}(0), then x=y=\mathsf{Obs}(0).
2. If x\times y=\mathsf{Obs}(0), then x=\mathsf{Obs}(0) or y=\mathsf{Obs}(0).
3. For every r,j,x: \mathsf{Miss}(r)\boxplus x=\mathsf{Miss}(r) and \mathsf{NA}(j)\boxplus x=\mathsf{NA}(j).
4. No rule maps \mathsf{Miss} or \mathsf{NA} to a numeric constructor.

Proof. By case analysis on the constructors in Table B.1.

Case (1). Suppose x+y=\mathsf{Obs}(0) where x,y\in\mathsf{M} are non-negative. Examining the table, the only rule producing an \mathsf{Obs}-constructor result is the (\mathsf{Obs},\mathsf{Obs}) cell. All other cells produce \mathsf{Cen}, \mathsf{Est}, \mathsf{Miss}, or \mathsf{NA}, none of which equals \mathsf{Obs}(0). Hence x=\mathsf{Obs}(a) and y=\mathsf{Obs}(b) with a+b=0. Since a,b\ge 0, a=b=0. \square

Case (2). The only rule producing an \mathsf{Obs}-constructor result is the (\mathsf{Obs},\mathsf{Obs}) cell. Hence x=\mathsf{Obs}(a), y=\mathsf{Obs}(b) with ab=0. An integral domain, \mathbb{R} has no zero divisors, so a=0 or b=0. \square

Case (3). In Table B.1, every cell in the \mathsf{Miss}(r) row and column returns \mathsf{Miss} (with the reason union where appropriate). Symmetrically for \mathsf{NA}(j). \square

Case (4). The only rules with numeric-constructor consequents have numeric-constructor premises (the first three rows and first three columns of the table). No rule with a \mathsf{Miss} or \mathsf{NA} premise has a numeric consequent. \square

