B.3 Interval arithmetic: monotonicity and soundness

Lemma B.2 (Endpoint propagation for + and non-negative \times). Let f:\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^2\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} be + or \times. For non-negative a and interval [l,h]\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}, the image f(\{a\}\times[l,h]) equals [f(a,l),f(a,h)].

Proof. Both + and \times are continuous and non-decreasing in each argument on \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}. Therefore f(a,\cdot) attains its minimum at l and maximum at h on [l,h]. \square

Lemma B.3 (Minkowski sum). For non-negative intervals [l_1,h_1] and [l_2,h_2], the Minkowski sum is [l_1+l_2,h_1+h_2].

Proof. For x_1\in[l_1,h_1] and x_2\in[l_2,h_2], x_1+x_2\in[l_1+l_2,h_1+h_2]. Conversely, every s\in[l_1+l_2,h_1+h_2] is realised by x_1=\max(l_1,\min(h_1,s-l_2)) and x_2=s-x_1. \square

Lemma B.4 (Minkowski product, non-negative). For non-negative intervals, the set \{x_1 x_2 : x_1\in[l_1,h_1], x_2\in[l_2,h_2]\} equals [l_1 l_2, h_1 h_2].

Proof. Non-negativity gives l_1 l_2\le x_1 x_2\le h_1 h_2. Every p\in[l_1 l_2,h_1 h_2] is attained: if l_1>0, set x_1=l_1, x_2=p/l_1. If l_1=0, then p=0 is attained by x_1=0. \square

Proposition B.5 (Soundness of propagation). If x_1\in[\![x_1]\!] and x_2\in[\![x_2]\!] are true values inside their measured intervals, then for f\in\{+,\times\} and non-negative arguments, f(x_1,x_2)\in f([\![x_1]\!],[\![x_2]\!]).

Proof. Immediate from Lemmas B.2–B.4. \square

