Definition B.6. Let \mathsf{Obs}^\pm(v,u,\sigma,d) be a signed observation with v\in\mathbb{R}. The signed extension satisfies:

\mathsf{Obs}^\pm(a)-\mathsf{Obs}^\pm(b)=\mathsf{Obs}^\pm(a-b,u),

\mathsf{Obs}^\pm(a)\div\mathsf{Obs}^\pm(b)=\mathsf{Obs}^\pm(a/b,u)\quad\text{if }b\ne 0,

\mathsf{Obs}^\pm(a)\div\mathsf{Obs}^\pm(0)=\mathsf{Miss}(\mathrm{NU})\text{ with annotation } \textit{division-by-zero}.

The signed extension is used only when the model requires signed quantities. For the pilot, the unsigned representation is retained with the guard

\mathsf{Obs}(a)-\mathsf{Obs}(b)=
\begin{cases}\mathsf{Obs}(a-b,u), & a\ge b,\\ \mathsf{Miss}(\mathrm{NU}) \text{ with annotation}, & a<b.\end{cases}

Lemma B.7 (Interval subtraction soundness). For non-negative a and interval [l,h]\subseteq[0,a], a-[l,h]=[a-h,a-l].

Proof. The map x\mapsto a-x is decreasing, so the minimum of a-x on [l,h] is a-h and the maximum is a-l. \square

