Appendix C — Provenance: complete proofs and confluence

C.1 Proof of Theorem 5.2 (verifiable replay)

Setup. Let \mathcal{H} be SHA-256 over a canonical serialisation \mathrm{ser}:\mathsf{Payload}\to\Sigma^*. Let R be the replay function defined by:

R(E,S_o)=\mathrm{Apply}(e_n,\mathrm{Apply}(e_{n-1},\dots,\mathrm{Apply}(e_1,S_o)\dots)),

where \mathrm{Apply}(e,x) applies the transformation described by event e to x, using the parameters, software version, and configuration recorded in e.

Assumptions. (A1) S_o is immutable and reachable. (A2) Each event records \mathrm{id}_{\mathrm{in}}, \mathrm{id}_{\mathrm{out}}, \pi_{\mathrm{params}}, v_{\mathrm{sw}}, and configuration. (A3) Serialisation preserves sufficient precision, or an explicit tolerance \epsilon is recorded. (A4) The event chain is acyclic and terminates at S_o.

Theorem. Under (A1)–(A4), R(E,S_o)=T(o) and \mathcal{H}(\mathrm{ser}(R(E,S_o)))=\mathcal{H}(\mathrm{ser}(T(o))), where equality is exact when (A3) preserves precision and within \epsilon otherwise.

Proof. By induction on the length n of E. For n=0, R(\varnothing,S_o)=S_o=T(o) since no transformation has been applied. For the step, assume R(E_{n-1},S_o)=T_{n-1}(o) and consider E_n=E_{n-1}\cup\{e_n\}. By (A2) e_n records the transformation identifier t_{\mathrm{id}} and its parameters. By determinism of the software identified by v_{\mathrm{sw}}, applying e_n to T_{n-1}(o) yields T_n(T_{n-1}(o)). The recorded output hash \mathcal{H}(\mathrm{ser}(T_n(o))) equals the computed hash by (A2) and (A3) within \epsilon. Acyclicity (A4) guarantees the induction is well-founded, and (A1) guarantees the base payload is available. \square

Remark C.1. The induction is short because the substantive content is in the assumptions, not the proof. The earlier framing as a deep reconstruction theorem was misleading. The theorem is a replay soundness property: if the assumptions hold, replay reproduces the recorded output. The risk is in the parsing step (§C.5), not the induction.

C.2 Proof of Proposition 5.3 (provenance-preserving representation)

Definition C.2. Let \mathrm{ID}:\mathsf{Payload}\to\mathsf{Locator} be a locator map assigning to each retained payload a content-addressed identifier (\text{source-id},\text{path},\text{hash}). \mathrm{ID} is well-defined if each locator resolves to a unique payload.

Proposition. Define A(o)=(T(o),\mathrm{ID}_o,P_o,S_o). If \mathrm{ID} is well-defined and P_o preserves every transformation dependency, then A(o_1)=A(o_2)\Rightarrow o_1=o_2 within the same locator domain.

Proof. A(o_1)=A(o_2) gives \mathrm{ID}_{o_1}=\mathrm{ID}_{o_2}. By well-definedness, both resolve to the same payload S. Since each source payload is a single observation (by construction of the source store), o_1=o_2. The provenance graphs P_{o_1},P_{o_2} are equal by hypothesis. \square

Remark C.3. Injectivity of \mathrm{ID} is not required. What is required is that the map be well-defined: two distinct locators must not resolve to the same payload, and one locator must not resolve to two payloads. A PDF contains many observations, so locators must specify table, row, column, and cell (or an equivalent locator). The content hash of the located payload provides collision resistance.

C.3 Critical pair enumeration

Definition C.4. Let \mathcal{T}=\{T_{\mathrm{tax}},T_{\mathrm{unit}},\dots\} be a set of transformations, each acting on a subset of the observation coordinates. A critical pair is a pair (t_1,t_2)\in\mathcal{T}^2 and an observation o such that both t_1 and t_2 are defined on o and t_1 writes to a coordinate that t_2 reads, or vice versa. For each critical pair, the local confluence condition is

t_1(t_2(o))=t_2(t_1(o))\quad\text{for all such }o.

Critical pairs identified.

(CP-1) T_{\mathrm{tax}} vs T_{\mathrm{unit}}, disjoint case. T_{\mathrm{tax}} writes \{s,\kappa\} (taxonomic fields); T_{\mathrm{unit}} writes \{v,u,\beta\} and reads only those fields. No critical pair; commutation is trivial.

(CP-2) T_{\mathrm{tax}} vs T_{\mathrm{unit}}, moisture-dependent case. T_{\mathrm{unit}} performs fresh-to-dry conversion using moisture w, and w is cultivar-dependent: w=w(\kappa). T_{\mathrm{unit}} therefore reads \kappa, which T_{\mathrm{tax}} writes. Critical pair exists. Local confluence requires

T_{\mathrm{unit}}(T_{\mathrm{tax}}(o))=T_{\mathrm{tax}}(T_{\mathrm{unit}}(o)).

This holds if T_{\mathrm{tax}} does not change w, i.e., if the synonymy edge preserves the moisture value. It fails if T_{\mathrm{tax}} resolves an ambiguous identification and the resolved taxon has a different cultivar-specific moisture.

(CP-3) T_{\mathrm{tax}} vs T_{\mathrm{tax}}. Multiple taxonomic transformations applied in sequence. Local confluence is required if two different normalization routes lead to the same accepted name. In practice, the WCVP accepted name is the unique normal form, so this critical pair is not genuinely critical for well-formed inputs.

(CP-4) T_{\mathrm{unit}} vs T_{\mathrm{unit}}. Two unit conversions. Local confluence holds if and only if the conversion factors are path-independent. For fresh-to-dry followed by unit conversion, they act on disjoint coordinates and commute; for two unit conversions in the same basis, they compose.

C.4 Newman's lemma and conditional confluence

Definition C.5. A rewrite system (\mathsf{M},\to) is terminating if there is no infinite rewrite sequence. It is locally confluent if every critical pair is joinable. It is confluent if every pair of divergent reductions has a common reduct.

Lemma C.6 (Newman, stated). A terminating rewrite system is confluent if and only if it is locally confluent.

Proposition C.7 (Conditional confluence of the pilot system). Suppose the pilot transformation system is terminating (finite event sequence, decreasing measure on unfixed coordinates) and the only critical pair is (CP-2). Then:

· On the subdomain where w does not depend on \kappa, the system is confluent.
· On the subdomain where w depends on \kappa and T_{\mathrm{tax}} modifies \kappa, the system is not confluent in general; the divergence is recorded, and the actual reduction order is preserved in the provenance graph.

Proof. Termination holds by finiteness of the event sequence. Local confluence holds by (CP-2) analysis on the first subdomain and fails by counterexample on the second. Newman's lemma gives confluence on the first subdomain and the absence of confluence on the second. \square

Counterexample C.8. Let o be a record with cultivar name “spinach” (ambiguous), wet-basis vitamin C 43.6 mg/100 g, and moisture 0.917. Suppose T_{\mathrm{tax}} resolves “spinach” to Spinacia oleracea (moisture 0.917) and T_{\mathrm{unit}} uses moisture. Then:

· T_{\mathrm{unit}}(T_{\mathrm{tax}}(o)): dry-basis vitamin C =43.6/0.083=525.3.
· If T_{\mathrm{tax}} had resolved to a different variety with moisture 0.900, then T_{\mathrm{unit}}(T_{\mathrm{tax}}(o))=43.6/0.100=436.0.

The two routes yield different values. Confluence fails. The provenance graph records the actual order and the resolved cultivar.

C.5 The potato parsing error as a provenance violation

The pilot's potato row in the original table copied 106 mg/kg into both winter and spring columns. This is a parsing-level error, not a transformation-level error. Under the framework:

1. The source table has one cell for winter/spring (pooled).
2. The parser created two cells, one for winter and one for spring, by copying.
3. The locator for the winter cell and the locator for the spring cell point to the same source cell.
4. Content-addressing detects this: both cells resolve to the same source content, and the well-definedness check flags a many-to-one mapping from locator to source.
5. The separation invariant check flags that the copy invents an observation.

The correct representation is a single record with sampling_season: winter/spring (pooled), not two records. This is the type of error the framework is designed to detect, and it did not detect it in the original table. The corrected Appendix F.1 records the pooling explicitly.

