Appendix E — Partial identification of harvest windows

E.1 Bounds under maximum storage lag

Assumption E.1. Let S_{\max} be the maximum time between harvest and sale for a specified product-market channel. For a purchase at time t, the harvest time \tau_h satisfies

\tau_h\in[t-S_{\max},t].

Proposition E.2. Under E.1, the harvest window B is contained in [t-S_{\max},t] for every purchase time t\in M.

Proof. Immediate from E.1. \square

E.2 Bounds under minimum and maximum lag

Assumption E.3. In addition to E.1, let s_{\min}\ge 0 be the minimum time between harvest and sale (e.g., transport and distribution time). Then

\tau_h\in[t-S_{\max},t-s_{\min}].

Proposition E.4. Under E.3, the harvest window B is contained in [t-S_{\max},t-s_{\min}] for every purchase time t\in M. The width of the interval is S_{\max}-s_{\min}.

Proof. Immediate. \square

E.3 Testable implications

Implication E.5 (Support condition). If M is the support of purchase times for a product in a market, then

M\subseteq B+[s_{\min},S_{\max}],

where B+[s_{\min},S_{\max}] denotes the Minkowski sum. This is testable against external harvest calendars. Failure indicates either that S_{\max} is larger than assumed, that s_{\min} is smaller than assumed, or that M includes re-exports or imports from other regions.

Implication E.6 (Bound on B from M). Under E.3, for every t\in M, the harvest window B is contained in [t-S_{\max},t-s_{\min}]. Intersecting over all t\in M:

B\subseteq\bigcap_{t\in M}[t-S_{\max},t-s_{\min}]=[\max(M)-S_{\max},\min(M)-s_{\min}].

If \max(M)-S_{\max}>\min(M)-s_{\min}, the intersection is empty and the assumptions are inconsistent with the data.

E.4 Worked example with the pilot

For spinach in the Blacksburg market, with S_{\max}=14 days (a plausible maximum for fresh spinach in retail) and s_{\min}=1 day:

· Winter sample: t=\text{winter}. Bounds on harvest: [\text{winter}-14,\text{winter}-1].
· Spring sample: bounds [\text{spring}-14,\text{spring}-1].
· Summer/fall sample: bounds [\text{summer/fall}-14,\text{summer/fall}-1].

The recorded state is harvest_window: [t-S, t] with S: Miss(NR) since the source does not document S_{\max}. The partial identification is recorded as an unresolved interval rather than a point value. The framework's contribution is to make this unresolvedness explicit and auditable.

